//? Title: Circle Layouts: Pair Relaxation and Measured Contacts
//? Subtitle: Actual working disks, normalization, tangency errors and bounded execution
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given an undirected graph, draw disks whose interiors do not overlap and whose
tangencies represent its edges. Avoiding overlap alone is insufficient: every
edge must also be a contact, and every nonedge must avoid contact. We inspect
datamunge's legacy equal-centre relaxation, retain the original triangle, and
measure what the returned disks actually represent. Native snapshots expose
the simultaneous pair updates, while separate disk diagnostics reveal lost
tangencies and an overlapping eight-cycle. An explicit variable-radius K4
packing and a two-circle closed form provide independent geometric controls.
)

\toc

> One actual pair update

The legacy function is called circle_packing_layout, but its numerical task is
more modest than constructing a graph's circle packing. It starts unit-radius
working disks on a circle of radius three. An edge attracts its centres towards
distance two; a nonedge repels centres closer than 2.15. Every pair reads the
same old positions, and all accumulated displacements are committed together.
This is a Jacobi sweep, with default step size η=0.08.

For two vertices joined by one edge, the initial distance is six. Each centre
moves η(6−2)=0.32 towards the other, leaving distance 5.36. The two rows below
are separate time panels in identical coordinate units; vertical separation is
only presentation. The figure uses actual captured centres and unit radii.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/circle_pair_step.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::algorithms::CirclePackingOptions options;
  options.iterations=1;options.snapshot_stride=1;
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::circle_packing_trace(2,{{0,1}},options);
  dm::graphics::Diagram figure(85,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke line;line.dashed=true;line.color={100,116,139};
  std::print("| actual sweep | centre distance | measured energy | maximum movement |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t row=0;row<trace.frames.size();++row) {
    const auto& frame=trace.frames[row];const double y=3-6.0*row;
    const auto a=frame.circles[0].center,b=frame.circles[1].center;
    const auto distance=std::hypot(a.x-b.x,a.y-b.y);
    std::print("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |\n",frame.iteration,distance,frame.energy,frame.max_displacement);
    figure.path({{a.x,a.y+y},{b.x,b.y+y}},line);
    for(std::size_t vertex=0;vertex<frame.circles.size();++vertex) {
      const auto& circle=frame.circles[vertex];
      figure.ellipse({circle.center.x,circle.center.y+y},circle.radius,circle.radius,{219,234,254})
            .text({circle.center.x,circle.center.y+y},std::to_string(vertex),22);
    }
    figure.text({0,y+1.6},std::format("sweep {}: d={:.2f}, energy={:.4f}",frame.iteration,distance,frame.energy),21);
  }
  figure.text({0,6},"One simultaneous sweep: each centre moves 0.32",23)
        .text({0,-5.1},std::format("Returned disks: radius 0.45, edge gap {:.2f}",trace.output_metrics.min_gap),21)
        .save_svg("build/circle_pair_step.svg");
  if(std::fabs(trace.frames.back().energy-5.6448L)>1e-10L)return 1;
}
```
