> The force rule and its limits

For raw centre distance d_uv, let the edge target be 2 and the nonedge
clearance target be 2.15. The measured potential is

$$
\Phi(p)=\frac12\sum_{uv\in E}(d_{uv}-2)^2
       +\frac12\sum_{uv\notin E}\max(0,2.15-d_{uv})^2.
$$
\alttext(The potential is half the sum of squared edge distance errors and squared nonedge clearance deficits, each unordered pair counted once.)

For each active pair, with target a_uv, the kernel adds

$$
\Delta p_u=\eta(d_{uv}-a_{uv})\frac{p_v-p_u}{d_{uv}},
\qquad \Delta p_v=-\Delta p_u.
$$
\alttext(An active pair contributes equal and opposite centre displacements, proportional to its signed distance error.)

Nonedges at or above their clearance target contribute zero. The implementation
uses max(1e−9,d_uv) in the force formula, including its target difference; the
gradient interpretation above applies away from that floor. Coincident centres
have zero direction and need not separate. Fixed steps have no line search,
adaptive damping, early convergence test or universal energy-descent guarantee.
Coordinates and aggregates use checked widened arithmetic before finite-double
coordinate storage. That can change last bits relative to the old unchecked path.

All endpoints are validated. Self-loops are ignored, and duplicate or reversed
edges collapse to one unweighted edge. There is no planarity check. In particular,
these updates do not implement the variable-radius construction underlying the
circle-packing theorem for finite simple planar graphs.

> The original triangle: raw tangencies disappear at output

For three mutually adjacent vertices, symmetry keeps the centres on an
equilateral triangle. At the raw limit their centre distance is two, and the
unit working disks touch. The API then subtracts the mean, divides centres by
their largest centred norm s, and resets every radius to 0.45:

$$
p'_i=(p_i-\bar p)/s,\qquad r'_i=0.45.
$$
\alttext(Centres are translated and scaled to unit extent, while every output radius is reset to 0.45 rather than divided by the same scale.)

A raw edge with distance two therefore has output gap 2/s−0.9. This is not a
similarity transformation of the disks, because radii are not scaled with the
centres. For the triangle s=2/√3, giving returned centre distance √3 and a
positive gap √3−0.9≈0.832050808. The original report checked only non-overlap;
that check cannot establish the edge tangencies it claimed.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/circle_triangle.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t,std::size_t>> edges{{0,1},{1,2},{2,0}};
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::circle_packing_trace(3,edges);
  const auto& raw=trace.frames.back().circles;
  const auto raw_metrics=dm::algorithms::circle_packing_metrics(raw,edges);
  std::print("| vertex | returned x | returned y | returned radius |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<trace.circles.size();++i) {
    const auto& c=trace.circles[i];std::print("| {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.2f} |\n",i,c.center.x,c.center.y,c.radius);
  }
  std::print("\n| geometry | overlaps | edge contacts / 3 | minimum gap | edge stress |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  dm::graphics::Diagram figure(95,1100);
  for(int panel=0;panel<2;++panel) {
    const auto& circles=panel?trace.circles:raw;
    const auto m=panel?trace.output_metrics:raw_metrics;const double shift=6.0*panel;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} |\n",panel?"returned disks":"raw working disks",m.overlapping_pairs,m.edge_contacts,m.min_gap,m.edge_stress);
    for(std::size_t i=0;i<circles.size();++i) {
      const auto& c=circles[i];
      figure.ellipse({c.center.x+shift,c.center.y},c.radius,c.radius,panel?dm::RGB{254,226,226}:dm::RGB{220,252,231})
            .text({c.center.x+shift,c.center.y},std::to_string(i),20);
    }
    figure.text({shift,3.3},panel?"returned centres\nradii reset to 0.45":"raw working disks\nradii remain 1",21)
          .text({shift,-3.3},std::format("{} edge contacts, {} overlaps",m.edge_contacts,m.overlapping_pairs),19);
  }
  figure.save_svg("build/circle_triangle.svg");
  std::print("\nActual normalization scale: {:.12f}. Completed sweeps: {}.\n",trace.normalization_scale,trace.work.completed_iterations);
  if(raw_metrics.edge_contacts!=3 || trace.output_metrics.edge_contacts!=0 || trace.output_metrics.overlapping_pairs!=0)return 1;
}
